TIP:  A couple of tricky bits to be aware of.  First is the sing-song (Cadd9)-(C).  Not hard, just requires a little concentration.  Hold (C) the whole time and add your pinky to the 2nd string/3rd fret to cover the <d> and strum the bottom four strings.  On off.

TIP:  Next, watch for the (CaddG) which is like the (Cadd9) but your pinky goes on the 1st string of the 3rd fret <g> instead.  It comes at the beginning of the last line of the second verse on the word “Six”.  It comes ‘round again in the verse that follows the instrumental bridge.

TIP:  If transposing, play the root chord of the transposed (CaddG) and (Cadd9) and play the 5th/9th note as necessary.

TIP:  Finally, the chorus chord progression is a simple (Am)(G)(F)(C).  Stick with it and you’ll be fine.  It does vary just a smidge in the final chorus as the (F) drags and the (C) is omitted.
 

